Yes, the Djinn do all seem a little bit to helpful, which seems far
away from the traditionally 'trickster' role they seem to have in some
of the tales they appear in.  They should probably be taking a few
more opportunities to rather liberally misinterpret whatever you wish
for.  The same for water demons.  Wishes from wands can probably
remain as they are.

>>Blessed items.  Where blessed items now do the same thing
>as unblessed items, make them just a little bit better.  Not enough
>to have any significant gameplay effect, but enough to make spoiled
>adventurers eagerly bless everything in sight (and possibly run low
>on holy water).

>I really dig this idea of entering Gehennom being one massive curse in
>itself.  Coming back out though perhaps shouldn't be?  Really, only
>the descent into hell would be a curse unto itself... exiting is more
>of a blessing (although shouldn't bless your items or anything).

Combine this with the earlier point about blessed items.  When you
enter Gehennom, anything not blessed, gets disenchanted (a single
step).  If fully disenchanted it becomes cursed.  If cursed, it
becomes corroded or burnt, potions may curdle, scrolls may crumble to
dust, magic items may become normal items, wands may loose charges,
gems may turn to glass.

